# harness nobody had reviewed for shape. docs/refactoring-backlog.md EX1
linux/llm-stack/bench_agent.py | main | cc | 27 | The 27 are 10 ifs (every one a guard — seven of them end in `raise SystemExit` or `return`), 3 IfExp defaults (`0 if self_test() else 1`, `[] if base_url else ["base_url"]`, `args.label or args.model or "default model"`), 3 BoolOps (that same `or` chain counts 2, plus the two `(config or {})` guards at 1072-1073), 6 comprehension ifs (the --task filter at 1013, the missing-tools filter at 1019, the drop at 1021, and the three scoring generators at 1047/1048/1051) and 5 loops/try — with nesting of only 2, so nothing interleaves and each decision is independent of the others. It is the same debt as the 106-line row, not a separate shape: the _select_tasks extraction removes exactly 7 points (the 1013 comprehension and its `or`, `if not tasks`, the 1019 and 1021 filters, the announce loop, the 1025 exit) and the _write_agent_report extraction removes 5 (`if args.output`, the base_url ternary, `if note`, the two `(config or {})`), landing main at 15 — the gate's limit, which is not exceeded — with the helpers at about cc 8 and cc 6. Same safety net, same nothing blocking it.
linux/llm-stack/bench_agent.py | old_name_uses | cc | 23 | Ten of the 23 are a five-arm node-kind table: ast.Name with node.id == name -> 'name', ast.Attribute with node.attr -> 'attribute', FunctionDef/AsyncFunctionDef/ClassDef with node.name -> 'definition', ast.alias matching node.name or node.asname -> 'import', and ast.Constant equal to name whose id() is not in the docstrings set -> 'string' (the arm that lets a `globals()['fetch_data']` lookup be caught while a docstring mentioning the old name is not) — each arm is one isinstance plus one field test, so the five `and`s the counter charges are the table's own cells, not nesting. The other 13 are traversal and one refusal: the os.walk with its .git/__pycache__/.pytest_cache dirs filter, the .py filter, the with-open, the ast.parse try/except SyntaxError that falls back to a plain `name in src` substring scan so an unparsable file can never read as clean, the docstring-id comprehension with its two isinstance conjunctions, and the inner ast.walk. The seam is to lift the ladder into a module-level _use_kind(node, name, docstrings) -> label or None, leaving a walker at about cc 10 and a classifier at about cc 13, both under the limit; the payoff is that the table — the exact thing the multi_file_rename 'old name aliased' cheat attacks — becomes unit-testable directly instead of only through a temp directory. Nothing blocks it: TestRenameTaskDecidesOnTheTree already exercises all five arms plus the comment, docstring and unparsable cases against ba.old_name_uses by name.
linux/llm-stack/bench_agent.py | self_test | cc | 18 | Three independent proofs concatenated, each costing a loop, a 'needs a tool' skip guard and a try/finally rmtree: TASKS must be red unsolved and green under REFERENCE, each CHEATS entry must stay red AND produce its expected substring ('tests were modified', 'the tests do not catch', 'fetch_data still used'), and the clamp pair must show the fixture's own tests killing no CLAMP_MUTANTS while the reference tests kill all four. Seven of the 18 points are not control flow at all: three `and`s that ARE the assertions ((not before) and after; (not passed) and expected in detail) and four IfExp that only choose words for the printed row ('pass'/'fail', 'cheat was accepted', 'validated'/'IS BROKEN'), with two more coming from the `next(t for t in TASKS if ...)` lookups; nesting is 3. The mechanical split into three phase helpers exists but buys nothing a reader can use: it moves the same 18 points into three rows and forces the `skipped` dict — today the reason a host without cmake prints 'SKIPPED and NOT checked' in the summary rather than a clean validation, which TestSkipsAreVisible pins — to become a value threaded from phase one into phase two. Note that swapping the three try/finally blocks for a `scratch_repo` context manager would shorten the function but not the count: the gate charges ast.With and a bare ast.Try one point each.
linux/llm-stack/bench_coding.py | _bound_names | cc | 18 | 18 = 7 If + 4 comprehension + 3 BoolOp + 2 IfExp + 1 For over 26 code lines with a two-line docstring and nesting of only 3, and the number is Python's binding grammar rather than a tangle. Four arms name what binds: an ast.Name in Store or Del context, a FunctionDef/AsyncFunctionDef/ClassDef's own name (the name is bound HERE even though the body belongs to the nested scope, which is exactly what _scope_nodes stops at), an ast.arg, and an alias's asname-or-first-dotted-component. The rest is one (targets, values) pairing per binder form -- Assign/AnnAssign/AugAssign/NamedExpr, For/comprehension, and ast.arguments with posonlyargs+args+kwonlyargs against defaults+kw_defaults -- feeding the single job the docstring states: subtract `sorted = sorted`-style self-captures, which bind the builtin under its own name and are a USE of it, not an exemption from check_forbidden. Splitting the bound set from the captured set would mean two walks over the same _scope_nodes(scope) list, which is the only expensive part, and would put the capture rule away from the bindings it corrects. One thing a reader should know: this function has NO direct test -- grep finds zero references to _bound_names in linux/llm-stack/tests -- so it is exercised only through check_forbidden (7 test references) and _unbound_uses, and the arm most likely to rot unnoticed is one of the rarer binder forms such as AugAssign or kw_defaults.
linux/llm-stack/bench_coding.py | ask | cc | 19 | 19 = 10 If + 5 BoolOp + 1 For + 1 With + 1 Try over 49 code lines, and the metric is literal: it is the number of shapes an OpenAI-compatible SSE stream arrives in, one arm each, with no decision in the function beyond 'is this field present'. The arms are an `error:` line injected into an already-200 stream (dropped, it graded FAIL 'no code found in reply' -- D3 in docs/llm-benchmark-review-2026-09-05.md), a non-`data:` line, the `[DONE]` sentinel, a JSONDecodeError skip, a chunk-level `error` object, `usage` for prompt_tokens and completion_tokens, a possibly-empty `choices`, `finish_reason` (which is what lets a server-reported cut stop depending on counting deltas), `reasoning_content` on llama.cpp/vLLM versus `reasoning` on Ollama, `content`, and the two first-delta ttft guards. This is a wire-format table over a protocol this harness does not control, and it grows by one arm per dialect quirk discovered, which is the gate working. There is also nothing clean to extract: the loop mutates six locals -- ttft, parts, think_parts, prompt_tokens, completion_tokens, finish -- that a per-chunk helper would have to carry as an explicit state object, which is more machinery than the branch count costs.
linux/llm-stack/bench_coding.py | check_forbidden | cc | 19 | 19 = 6 If + 8 BoolOp + 2 For + 1 Try + 1 IfExp over 27 code lines under a 17-line docstring, and every branch is one route by which a candidate reached a name the prompt forbade -- a refusal matrix, where flattening an arm re-opens a hole that was real. The four reporting arms over ast.walk are not a chain but a set: a bare Name that no ENCLOSING scope binds (through _unbound_uses, because a file-wide scan let an unrelated helper's parameter named `sorted` whitelist every call in the file), an Attribute (`builtins.sorted`), an import alias, and a str Constant that _is_lookup_string proves is the argument to getattr/__import__/import_module/attrgetter/methodcaller or a subscript key into __builtins__/vars()/globals() -- each returning a differently worded verdict that names the bypass rather than a generic refusal. The remaining branches are the SyntaxError fallback to _check_forbidden_text (unparseable code will fail the tests anyway), the _FORBIDDEN_NAMES mapped-versus-unmapped token split, and the closing text scan that deliberately runs on the MAPPED tokens too, because a module-level `for sorted in []` binds the name without ever executing and would otherwise leave the builtin reachable. The whole apparatus exists because `return sorted(a + b)` satisfied every assertion of the merge task while doing exactly what the prompt forbade; the count is the number of documented bypasses, and it is correct as written.
linux/llm-stack/bench_coding.py | evaluate | cc | 69 | 69 = 19 If + 19 IfExp + 14 BoolOp + 11 comprehension + 3 For + 2 Try, four and a half times the limit of 15, and the reason it is that high rather than the usual double is that three DIFFERENT kinds of branch share one body. About 30 sit in the per-attempt block: the overflow-vs-errored split at 1622-1643 (_overflow_reason deciding whether a 4xx is a context overflow or an honest transport error), the skipped/gave_up/truncated/verdict cascade at 1655-1685, and eleven ternaries that build the printed columns (`suffix`, `pt`, `partial`, `unit`) and the row dict. About 21 sit in the aggregation: nine `sum(1 for r in results if r.get(...))` counters plus the `all(len(v) == 1 ...)` determinism and agreement tests. About 10 are in the four conditional summary prints, and 3 are `round(x, 2) if x else None` cells in the return dict. So the count is a fold, a verdict ladder and output formatting interleaved -- not one refusal matrix where flattening costs safety. Same seam as the 227-line row: _attempt_row takes the cascade and the formatting ternaries, _summarise takes the counters and the determinism test, and evaluate is left with the loop and the return. Nesting is already at the limit of 5 here, so this row and its neighbour are one job.
linux/llm-stack/bench_coding.py | extract_code | cc | 21 | 21 = 11 If + 5 comprehension + 2 BoolOp + 2 IfExp over 36 code lines and 24 comment lines, and most of it genuinely is a precedence ladder where the ORDER is the contract: strip everything before `</think>`, refuse outright when `<think>` never closed (a draft cut mid-thought was being graded PASS at the cap), then the fenced block that _defines the wanted symbol with its _is_preamble neighbours prepended, then any block containing a `def`, then the longest -- each rung carrying the mis-grade that put it there, written up as D1/D4/D5 in docs/llm-benchmark-review-2026-09-05.md. What is NOT a ladder is the tail. Lines 562-570 and 571-582 are the same five lines written twice: `m = re.search(<anchor>, text, ...)`, `if not m: return ""`, `tail = text[m.start():]`, `cut = re.search(r"^\s*```\s*$", tail, re.M)`, `return (tail[:cut.start()] if cut else tail).strip()` -- differing only in the anchor (_LANG_SHAPE[lang] versus the from/import/def/class/assignment alternation). The seam is _bare_code(text, anchor): it deletes a two-copy clone and one `if not m` arm. Be honest about the arithmetic -- cc lands near 19 and the row survives, and the three scattered `if lang != "python"` tests at 546/555/562 stay -- so the reason to cut is the duplicate, not the number. 33 test assertions and 4 mutations.json rows cover this function, so nothing blocks it.
linux/llm-stack/bench_coding.py | main | cc | 31 | 31 = 15 If + 6 For + 4 BoolOp + 3 comprehension-ifs + 2 IfExp, and 19 of the 30 branch points land inside the 72-line ranking block at 1978-2049 alone: the is_control partition and its `or reports` fallback, the tier loop and its separator line, the per-row effective_n/effective_k cells, the sorted groups union, the per-lang/per-kind matrix with its three cell arms ('-' for absent, 'skipped' for measured==0, k/n otherwise), and the three conditional footnotes. The 50-line argparse block above contributes exactly zero -- it is a flag table, the shape this repo's allow file already calls 'a TABLE, not tangle'. So this row and the 181-line row have ONE seam between them, print_ranking(reports, suspect), which takes main to roughly cc 12 and deletes the row rather than re-baselining it. What is left after the cut -- the five-arm --task-set dispatch, `if args.output`, `if len(reports) > 1` -- is a driver's normal shape and would be under the limit.
linux/llm-stack/bench_compare.py | _recount_groups | cc | 17 | Two paths over the limit, and the body is three flat, independent re-derivations rather than a tangle: the `for field, group_key in (('by_kind','kind'), ('by_lang','lang'))` table loop that classifies each row three ways (skipped / not measured -> excluded / measured, incrementing `passed` on the last), the `categories` recount over `kept`, and the wall statistics block. Five of the paths are `if field not in report` / `if 'categories' in report` / `if 'total_wall_s' in report` / `if 'median_wall_s' in report` / `if 'stdev_wall_s' in report` presence guards, and those guards are the contract, not slack: they exist so a re-derivation never INVENTS a table on a report shape whose producer never wrote one, and the remaining `if walls else None` / `if len(walls) > 1 else None` are the empty-sample refusals that stop statistics.median/stdev raising on a report where every row was suspect. The apparent duplication — the group loop re-walks `rows` and re-applies `_case_key(r) in dropped` even though `kept` is already a parameter — is required, because by_kind/by_lang must still count the skipped and unmeasured rows that `kept` deliberately excludes. A cut into `_regroup`/`_recount_categories`/`_recount_walls` is possible but buys nothing: each block is already labelled by the report field it writes, none reads another's output, and the function exists precisely because this block was extracted out of mark_suspect_cases already.
linux/llm-stack/bench_compare.py | compare | cc | 63 | Same seam as the 182-line row, and the distribution proves it is not one hard decision but four stacked ones: 12 of the 63 paths sit in the preamble (benchmark-name mismatch, the compare_provenance loop, the changed_cfg comprehension, the suspect-set banner, the two missing/new label loops) and 51 sit in the per-label loop, split 18 for the per-case section, 16 for the aggregate score, 8 for timing and 8 for throughput+serialised. Individual clusters here ARE a refusal matrix and would survive any split — `single_draw` (a one-attempt flip on a lane nobody showed deterministic is named, not alarmed), `strict = _known_deterministic(a) and _known_deterministic(b)`, the `effective_k is None -> min(a_n, round(a_rate*a_n))` legacy clamp, and the `if 'legacy' in (a_src, b_src)` timing caveat each map to a named past wrong verdict — but 63 is the SUM of four such matrices that never interact, not the cost of one. Extracting the four per-label helpers leaves compare() at roughly its preamble count (~12) and puts each matrix at 8-18 in a function whose name says which verdict it defends; the honest allow row after that split would cover `_case_findings` and `_score_findings` on refusal-matrix grounds, not this 63.
linux/llm-stack/bench_compare.py | mark_suspect_cases | cc | 16 | One path over the limit, on a function whose expensive half has already been extracted — `_recount_groups` at line 478 is the seam that would otherwise be named here, and it was taken. What is left is the headline recount, and the count is dominated by the two-condition kept/dropped genexps that literally define 'kept': `[r for r in rows if measured(r) and _case_key(r) not in dropped]` and the matching `sum(1 for r in rows if measured(r) and _case_key(r) in dropped)` contribute four paths between them. The rest are refusals, each with a stated failure it prevents: the `if not suspect: return suspect` early exit that keeps None (no control ran) distinguishable from [] (a control that failed nothing) for the caller, the `if is_control(report): report['suspect_excluded'] = 0; continue` arm that keeps the calibration entry scored in full so it is not silently graded as a competitor, `if 'wrong' in report` so a field the producer never wrote is not created, and the `if report.get('deterministic')` fork that recomputes effective_n/effective_k over `(case, variant)` outcome sets on a deterministic lane versus plain kept/passed otherwise — the branch that stops repeats on a deterministic endpoint being counted as independent trials. tests/test_bench_review_fixes.py (225-298) and test_bench_integration.py (184-260) pin all four arms including the None-vs-[] distinction, so the branches are load-bearing and observed, not residue.
linux/llm-stack/bench_compare.py | normalise | cc | 20 | The metric is close to literal here and falls out of the size row rather than standing on its own: five paths over the limit, and every one is an optional-field fallback in a field-mapping table, not a decision about the run. The count is the guard BoolOp plus the `if 'reports'` fork, the two nested loops, `key is None or not measured(item)` (2), the `isinstance(item.get('wall_s'), (int, float))` check, `label = r.get('label') or r.get('model')`, the duplicate-label `any(...)` genexp, the two-condition probe inheritance, the `{k: (sum(v), len(v))}` comprehension, and in the legacy branch the `'error' not in r` / `latency_s is not None` filters and `round(sum(walls), 2) if walls else None` — i.e. one path per report-shape variant this suite has actually emitted (wall_measured_s, effective_k, determinism_probe and median_wall_s are all absent in older reports and each absence has a documented consequence in the comments at 98-111). No branch here produces a verdict; compare() does that. Extracting `_entry_from_report` for the 92-line row moves roughly eight of these paths with it and drops the parent under the limit on its own, so there is no separate cut to make against this number.
linux/llm-stack/bench_embeddings.py | run | cc | 19 | The 19 is 1 + two loops (SIZES at 118, TRIPLES at 141) + four except handlers (tries at 100, 119, 129, 142) + the three-value BoolOp at 108 + eight ast.IfExp (109, 113, 114, 132, 134, 150, 157, 159) + the report['batch_speedup'] or 0 guard at 134, and ten of those points decide only the WORDING of a print: the ternaries choose 'deterministic' vs 'NOT deterministic', append the ', problems: ...' suffix, choose 'batches' vs 'little or no batching', print 'ok ' vs 'FAIL', and suppress the median-margin clause when margins is empty -- while the sole ast.If, at 161, guards nothing but a four-line NOTE print and never touches report, so contrary to what the raw count suggests there is exactly ONE branch in the whole function that changes the value run returns: the except at 103 that bails with {**report, 'error': str(e)[:200]} when the endpoint cannot even embed 'shape probe'. The other three except arms are one refusal per independent probe and are the function's actual contract -- a backend that 500s on the 4500-char 'long' text, or refuses ['batch probe']*8, leaves latency_s['long']=None or batch_speedup=None and still reaches the semantic triples, the one section a comparison harness cannot afford to skip. The seam is real and nameable: the latency, batch and semantics sections share only base_url/model/entry plus the report dict and would extract as _probe_latency/_probe_batch/_probe_semantics (the shape section is not their peer, it returns early), but the extraction relocates the count rather than removing it, since every ternary travels with the print it words -- run would still be 7 (base, the 100/103 try, the BoolOp at 108, the IfExp at 109/113/114) against 3/5/7 for the three probes, 22 points across four functions instead of 19 in one. And whoever attempts it works without a net: tests/test_bench_embeddings.py imports only cosine, check_shape and TRIPLES, and no other test under linux/llm-stack/tests references run or embed at all.
linux/llm-stack/bench_lanes.py | stream_once | cc | 17 | Debt, with a seam that lands precisely on the limit. By verify_code_complexity.py's own counter the 17 is: base 1 + Try@66 + With@67 + For@68 + If@69 + its `deadline and` BoolOp + If@74 (`data:`) + If@77 (`[DONE]`) + Try@79 (JSONDecodeError) + BoolOp@83 (`or []`) + If@84 + its BoolOp + If@85 (`ttft is None`) + BoolOp@92 (`ttft or 0`) + IfExp@94 (round-or-None) + IfExp@98 (decode rate) + BoolOp@99 (`decode_window > 0 and tokens > 1`). Routing the request through `bench_cli.post_json(f"{base_url}/v1/chat/completions", body, entry=entry, stream=True, timeout=..., deadline=...)` and iterating `resp.lines()` — exactly how bench_coding.py:1478 and benchmark_openai_api.py:507 already read the same stream — removes exactly TWO of those points, the If@69 and its BoolOp, because `Response.lines()` (bench_cli.py:205) owns the deadline check and `resp.gave_up`; the `with`/`for` remain branch points under different names, the `.decode("utf-8","replace").strip()` was never a branch, and the outer Try must stay since post_json lets HTTPError propagate and tests/test_bench_lanes.py:82 pins that a dead lane returns `{"error": ...}` instead of raising. Two is the whole win: 17-2 = 15, CC_LIMIT is 15 and check_counts only flags `n > limit`, so the correct follow-up is DELETING this row, not re-freezing it at 15 (a re-freeze fails as a STALE freeze). Nothing blocks the extraction — bench_cli is already imported in main() for write_report, and tests/test_bench_lanes.py drives a real ThreadingHTTPServer stub that post_json satisfies unchanged — and it closes a live gap rather than only moving code: resolve_lane returns only (name, url, model) and discards the registry entry while stream_once hardcodes `headers={"Content-Type": "application/json"}`, so request_headers/request_extras never run and any backends.json backend with api_key_env, per-entry headers or request_extra is unbenchable through both --lanes and --batching, coming back as `{"error": "HTTPError: ... 401"}`. The 15 that remain must NOT be flattened: If@74/If@77/Try@79/BoolOp@83/If@84/If@85 are the wire format's own arms, the space-optional `data:` prefix pinned in both directions by tests/test_bench_lanes.py:75 and tests/test_harness_against_ollama.py:85, and the four points at 92/94/98/99 are literal None- and zero-division guards for the HTTP-200-with-zero-tokens case that _secs's docstring records as having taken down a whole lane run on the QAIRT lane.
linux/llm-stack/bench_sweep.py | sweep | cc | 16 | One path over the limit of 15, and the fifteen decision points are exactly the five phases' local logic rather than any single knot: line 154 `for cand in candidates`, 155 `if args.skip_gate`, the 159 try with two handlers (the SystemExit re-raise that keeps a missing-API-key message from being buried, plus the BLE001 catch that turns a dead lane into an `unreachable` verdict), the 168 IfExp that only formats "(score/total)", 176 `for tool, cand, path in steps`, 178 the unreachable skip, the 194 IfExp choosing "ok"/"failed", 197 `if rc != 0`, the 201 comprehension filter `if s.get("written")`, the two `or` fallbacks in the manifest argv (206 `args.title or`, 207 `c["model"] or "?"`), 214 `if args.baseline`, 216 `for path in written`, and the 227 `with open`. Not one of those branches is removable — three of them are the refusal logic this driver exists for, and the rest are argv defaults and status-string formatting — so this is not independent complexity to delete but the same debt as the 97-line row seen through a second metric: the gate_all / run_steps / report-tail extraction named there relocates the branches and leaves sweep near cc 3 with every helper below six, and no other change would move this number without deleting a safeguard.
linux/llm-stack/bench_tools.py | _grade | cc | 18 | a refusal matrix: every branch is one way this function must NOT hand out a pass. 647-650 is the accept_text_json salvage, which also sets `recovered` so a text-channel rescue can never be counted as a native tool call; 652-657 is the restraint arm, and its `if not (message.get("content") or "").strip(): return False, "empty reply"` is D20 -- an endpoint answering HTTP 200 with zero tokens used to score 3/27 "correctly answered without a tool" -- pinned by the mutation tools.restraint-passes-on-silence; 661-663 refuses a no-call reply and echoes 60 characters of it so the failure is attributable rather than anonymous; 664-665 is the exact-count check for the parallel cases, pinned by tools.parallel-count-unchecked; 667-677 is the order-free match over `unmatched`, whose blame rule at 671-674 ("blame the call that reached for this tool, else the first one") exists so the detail string names the right call when several are wrong. The one cut available is a _match_expects over 667-677, worth about 5 paths, which would take this to ~13 and delete the row -- and it is not worth taking: `len(calls) != len(expects)` and the per-expect identity match are one contract about the same parallel case, and separating them puts the count verdict and the identity verdict in different functions while the caller still has to reassemble them. 35 lines, well under the size limit, and it reads top to bottom as one verdict ladder.
linux/llm-stack/bench_tools.py | evaluate | cc | 53 | the same three-subject body as the 161-line row, counted a second way, and the count needs discounting before it is believed: 16 of the 53 paths are inline ternaries and 5 more are comprehension ifs, most of them formatting rather than decisions (the three `round(...) if walls else None` at 978-980, the stdev tail at 954, the err_note/rec_note at 942-943, the suffix and PASS/FAIL formats at 869, 879-880, 889, 903-904), leaving 8 for, 11 if and 3 try as real control flow. That discount does not rescue the row, because the paths are not one table -- by region they are 4 in the head, 13 in the single-turn loop, 11 in the multi-turn loop and 25 in the aggregate-and-report block, three separable regions each carrying a double-digit count of its own. Same seam, same cost and same coverage as the function row: _run_single_cases 14, _run_multi_cases 12, _summary_stats 15, _print_summary 9, evaluate 4, with the five mutations.json `find` strings inside 855-981 re-anchored in the same commit. What must NOT be done is shaving the ternaries to buy the number down: they are the report's rounding, the "(N recovered from text)" note and the "attempt(s) EXCLUDED (transport errors)" note, and deleting them would leave three subjects in one body behind a green metric.
linux/llm-stack/bench_tools.py | grade_error_recovery | cc | 16 | one path over the limit, and its seams have already been cut -- the five things it needs are five named helpers directly above it: _call_key (compare a call by name plus canonicalised args, so a retry of the same read with "./x" is still the same retry), _failed_calls (which calls in the history got an error result back), _own_words (strip fenced blocks, quote lines, backticks and any text copied from a tool message, so the model quoting the ENOENT is not credited with admitting it), _fence_bodies (a block with and without its language tag) and _quoted_from (is this block text already in the history). What is left in the body is the verdict table itself: re-issued an identical failed call = FAIL (774-776), retried something different = PASS (777), empty reply = FAIL (779-780), a fenced block that is not quoted from the history = invented content (781-786), nothing but quoted output = did not report the failure (788-789), ADMIT_RE hit in its own words = PASS (790-791), otherwise it answered over the error (792). Six of those arms carry their own mutations.json entry (tools.identical-retry-accepted, tools.retry-key-ignores-path-spelling, tools.admit-words-unbounded, tools.quoted-output-counts-as-admission, tools.invented-fenced-block-accepted, tools.text-json-not-threaded-into-recovery), which is the evidence that each was separately earned; merging any two buys one path and re-opens a way for a model to pretend a failed read succeeded. Leave it -- 31 lines, and the cc is the size of the refusal table.
linux/llm-stack/bench_tools.py | main | cc | 21 | 13 of the 21 paths sit in the 1109-1147 ranking block -- the is_control filter and its comprehension at 1121, the `for x in reports if is_control(x)` calibration loop at 1123, the tier loop at 1134, the per-group row loop at 1137, `r.get("effective_n") or r["total"]` at 1140, the suspect note at 1113, the `len(reports) > 1` and `len(tiered) < len(ranked)` guards -- and the other 8 are the CLI surface: one guard per mode (`if args.system`, `if args.turn_growth`, `if args.output`) over 14 flags, which is the "cc counts the CLI" shape this repo already accepts. So this row is debt only because a second subject is sharing the body: lifting the ranking into bench_cli.print_ranking (see the 141-line row, where the same block is shown to be a clone of bench_coding.py:1978-2020) leaves main at 8 and deletes this row outright, while the extracted helper lands at 14, under the limit. Nothing blocks it -- print-only code, outside the build closure -- and the two rows are one edit.
linux/llm-stack/benchmark_openai_api.py | benchmark_chat | cc | 42 | Not a table and not a refusal matrix: the 42 paths are three unrelated jobs interleaved, and they partition cleanly. Roughly six belong to the warmup (the if warmup at 466, its try, the SystemExit re-raise that distinguishes a missing API key from a cold-start hiccup, and the extra_params merge); about fourteen belong to the SSE reader at 509-535 (data: prefix, [DONE], JSONDecodeError, the usage chunk, empty choices, piece from content-or-reasoning, and the first-token latch); and the remaining ~20 are derivation guards over local variables — the three usage.get ternaries at 563-565, the estimated-token fallback at 572-574, elapsed > 0 at 577, the ttft/decode/prefill ladder at 583-593, the </think> split at 600-603 and the three round-if-not-None ternaries inside the yielded dict at 617-619. Extracting _consume_sse and _derive_result (the same seam as the 183-line row) leaves each of the three under the limit of 15 and, unlike today, makes the derivation callable from a unit test without a socket. Outside the build closure.
linux/llm-stack/benchmark_openai_api.py | collect_hardware_info | cc | 32 | Close to literal but not quite: six try/except blocks contribute one path each (58, 69, 96, 108, 121, 130), and most of the rest is one branch per collected fact plus its fallback — the "model name" and "siblings" scans with their `if cpus else []` guards (74-90), the core-id set walk (80-85), the MemTotal scan (99-103), the three-way or at 111 and the five setdefault / `if not info.get(...)` fallback tests at 123-137. What keeps it from being a fair "the metric is literal" row is that the facts and their fallbacks are separated by fifty lines: cpu_physical_cores is derived at 84-85 and re-derived at 132-133, cpu_total_threads at 91 and 134-135, ram_total_gb at 102 and 136-137, so a reader has to hold two halves of the same decision in mind. The per-source split named on the 106-line row (_cpuinfo_facts / _meminfo_facts / _portable_facts merged by a small collector) puts each fact next to its own fallback and takes every piece under 15; the two duplicated cpus[0] scans also collapse into one pass over a field table. Nothing in tests/ covers this function, which is what the extraction should be paired with.
linux/llm-stack/benchmark_openai_api.py | main | cc | 28 | None of these 28 come from the CLI surface — argparse.add_argument is not a decision, so the thirteen flags at 882-908 contribute zero — which means the count is entirely the four inlined modes and their bookkeeping, and it double-counts state that a mode split would localise. Concretely: `json.loads(args.extra_params) if args.extra_params else None` is written twice, at 941 for the correctness-only path and again at 966 for the run; partial_path's ternary-and-exists dance appears at 973-975 and again at 1040-1041; the `interrupted` flag is tested three times (1003 gating the correctness probe, 1033 gating the output write, 1044 gating exit 130); and the correctness-only exit ladder at 947-951 is a second, independent verdict path over the same probe dict that print_correctness at 851-859 already classifies. Splitting out _show_saved / _correctness_only / _run (the seam on the 170-line row) puts each mode's guards in the function that owns them and drops all four well under 15; there is no separate action for this row.
linux/llm-stack/benchmark_openai_api.py | print_table | cc | 35 | Split the count honestly: about twelve of the 35 come from lines 791-796 alone, where six list comprehensions each contribute their comprehension `if` plus the `and` inside it — one predicate, "X" in r and "error" not in r, copy-pasted six times for latencies, tps_vals, cpu_vals, ram_vals, comp_tokens and total_tokens, and all six collapse into one shared ok = [r for r in results if "error" not in r]. That part is not irreducible branching, it is repetition the gate is correctly counting. The rest genuinely is literal — one path per optional summary line: the error arm in the row loop (772), `if latencies` (798), the token totals (804), the TTFT block and its "not measured — re-run with --stream" elif (811/819), decode (815), prefill (817), the busiest-process rollup (825-829) and the LB3 time-to-answer and thinking-share lines (835-839) — each of which prints if and only if the data carries that metric. So the actionable half is the filter, and it disappears with the same print_rows/print_summary split the 89-line row names.
linux/llm-stack/benchmark_openai_api.py | benchmark_chat | nesting | 8 | The eight levels are one straight chain and every one is real: for i, prompt (482) → try (503) → if stream (504) → with post_json (507) → for line in response.lines() (509) → if line.startswith("data:") (512) → try json.loads (516) → if "usage" in chunk / if piece / if piece and first_token_at is None (519, 527, 530). The decisive evidence that this is shape rather than necessity: the identical SSE loop is written twice more in the same directory — bench_lanes.py:74 and bench_coding.py:1488 — and both write it as the guard clause `if not line.startswith("data:"): continue`, which is why both sit shallow while this one nests positively. Inverting 512 the same way costs nothing and removes a level immediately; hoisting 507-535 into _consume_sse(response) -> (content, usage, streamed_chunks, first_token_at) takes benchmark_chat to depth 3 and the helper to about 4, both inside the limit of 5. This is the same seam as the cc-42 and 183-line rows, so it is one edit, not three. Note the three readers are not mergeable as they stand — bench_coding additionally handles `error:` lines, reasoning_content and finish_reason, bench_lanes counts content deltas only — so the fix here is invert-and-hoist inside this file, not a fourth shared parser.
linux/llm-stack/nas_census.py | extract_xlsx_text | nesting | 6 | The 6 is a hand-rolled zip walk stacked on a hand-rolled second copy of _local_texts: try(1) > with zipfile.ZipFile(2) > for name in sorted(names)(3) > if name.startswith("xl/worksheets/") and name.endswith(".xml")(4) > for el in root.iter()(5) > if el.tag.rpartition("}")[2] == "is"(6) at lines 249-259, where levels 5-6 re-type _local_texts's own element.iter() + tag.rpartition("}")[2] test with the local name "is" and the `el.text and` guard dropped, purely to reach the <is> wrappers before extending with _local_texts(el) — so the depth is duplication, not a decision tree. Only extract_docx_text is a true one-line delegate to _zip_xml_texts; extract_pptx_text (266-274) also opens the zip by hand for its sorted ppt/slides/slide* genexp and then lets _zip_xml_texts open the same file a SECOND time, so the seam fixes both: a _zip_members(zf, prefix, suffix) helper returning the sorted member names (killing the pptx genexp and its double-open at the same time) plus a _local_elements(root, "is") selector so the inline-string arm reads parts.extend(_local_texts(el) for el in _local_elements(root, "is")), collapsing this body to try > with > two flat loops at depth 3 while keeping the deliberately narrow <is> scoping (a blanket _local_texts(root) would sweep in any other t-tagged worksheet element) intact. What blocks it is narrower than nothing but is fixable in one test: tests/test_nas_census.py pins only the xlsx happy path (test_xlsx_reads_shared_strings_and_inline_strings, with make_xlsx writing both xl/sharedStrings.xml and xl/worksheets/sheet1.xml), while every None-arm test is written against the docx twin, and the twins genuinely diverge there — a valid zip with no xl/ members returns "" here where extract_docx_text returns None — so the refactor must first add an xlsx member-missing case pinning "", or it can silently move a file from 'empty text' to the walker's errors tally with no test going red.
linux/llm-stack/tools_opencode.py | translate_expect | cc | 17 | A one-to-one correspondence TABLE between two closed tool vocabularies, wrapped in element-wise recursion: the 17 is 1 + a 3-point recursion prologue (line 345 `expect is None`, line 347 `isinstance(expect, list)`, and the line 349 `UNTRANSLATABLE if any(...) else out` ternary; the comprehension and genexp cost 0 because `_py_paths` charges `len(node.ifs)` and they have none) + one equality arm for each of the 7 default-set names it can restate (read_file->read, search_code->grep, write_file->write, apply_patch->edit, run_tests/git_status/git_diff->bash) + 4 argument guards (`"max_lines" in args`, `set(args) == {"query"}`, `"diff" in args`, `set(args) <= {"path"}`) + 2 comprehension filters in the apply_patch arm that split `args["diff"]["contains"]` on leading -/+ into `removed`/`added`; that is 1+3+7+4+2 = 17, nesting 2, arms mutually exclusive and order-independent. The guards are load-bearing, not cosmetic, because the single terminal `return UNTRANSLATABLE` is what bench_tools.py:857 uses to SKIP a case rather than grade it: `search_code` with `case_sensitive` (opencode's grep has no case flag) or with `include` (grep's `include` is a string glob, `search_code`'s is an array -- a shape mismatch, not a missing parameter), or `run_tests` with `verbosity`, must reach the skip rather than be graded against a grep/bash expectation that quietly dropped the constraint; `"diff" in args` is the narrower case of a representability precondition, since `args["diff"]["contains"]` is the only source of oldString/newString. The arm set is currently right -- bench_tools.TOOLS has exactly 8 names and list_files is deliberately absent per the trailing comment, glob "*" and `bash ls` both being defensible -- but nothing enforces that: no test under linux/llm-stack/tests/ references translate_expect or UNTRANSLATABLE, so a ninth tool would silently join the skip bucket and show up only as a larger count in the bench_tools.py:949 print. Two seams exist. The `{name: callable}` dispatch dict is not worth taking: it relocates the 7 arms and their guards into 7 lambdas and breaks a correspondence table whose value is reading as one column against the 8-name default set, lowering the number without removing a decision. The real one is the prologue: hoisting lines 345-349 into a wrapper over a `_translate_one` splits list plumbing from the vocabulary table and leaves the table at cc 14, under the limit, deleting this row -- nothing blocks it except that no test covers translate_expect, so the split would ship unverified. The row stands only as long as that trade is made deliberately; take the wrapper the next time this function is touched.
